==English==
{{wikipedia}}

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|sa}} {{term|sc=Deva|देवनागरी|tr=Devánāgarī}}, compound of {{term|sc=Deva|देव|tr=devá||half-human — demi-divine|lang=sa}} + {{term|sc=Deva|नगर|tr=nágara|lang=sa||"to talk", or — dialect}}; literally “A dialect of Devas”.<ref>[http://translate.google.com/translate?js=n&prev=_t&hl=en&ie=UTF-8&layout=2&eotf=1&sl=ru&tl=en&u=http%3A%2F%2Ftraditio.ru%2Fwiki%2F%25D0%2594%25D0%25B5%25D0%25B2%25D0%25B0%25D0%25BD%25D0%25B0%25D0%25B3%25D0%25B0%25D1%2580%25D0%25B8 Devanagari]</ref><!--The name Devanagari should therefore be understood as “excellent or perfected form of the Nagari script”.-->—

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˌdeɪvəˈnɑːɡəriː/}}
* {{audio|en-us-Devanagari.ogg|Audio (US)}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj|-}}

# Of the Devanagari script or of a Devanagari alphabet.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|-}}

# An [[abugida]] script used to write several [[Indian]] languages, mainly [[Sanskrit]] and [[Hindi]], but also [[Marathi]], [[Kashmiri]], [[Sindhi]], [[Bihari]], [[Bhili]], [[Konkani]], and [[Bhojpuri]]. It is also used to write [[Nepali]] in [[Nepal]].

